# 8. A schema is a capability declaration

The previous seven tutorials built a protocol. This one and the next two build a
**query** — and they exist to make one distinction visible, because it is the
distinction the whole data-modeling argument turns on.

LinkML is the industry standard for modelling data. It is a YAML schema
language: you declare classes, you declare slots, and it compiles outward to
JSON Schema, SHACL, ShEx, OWL, RDF, SQL DDL, GraphQL, and typed code in half a
dozen languages. It is very good at what it does.

The claim these tutorials make is **not** that it is inadequate. It is narrower
and more precise, and it comes from *Shape Is Not Admissibility*:

> A LinkML schema **is** a capability declaration -- not analogously, but
> literally, as an instance of the same primitive.

So the question is not *LinkML or this*. It is: once you have a schema, what
have you got, and what have you not got? This tutorial answers the first half.
Tutorial 9 answers the second. Tutorial 10 puts them together.

## What a schema declares

Take a LinkML class with four slots:

```yaml
classes:
  Enzyme:
    slots: [ec_number, organism, substrate, k_cat]
```

Strip the syntax and what remains is a **set of qualified slot names** --
`(Enzyme, ec_number)`, `(Enzyme, organism)`, and so on. Call that set the
source's capability set, `Cap(Src)`. It is drawn from a finite alphabet of
features, and every feature in it has one property that matters:

**it is independently checkable.** You can decide whether a record has an
`ec_number` by looking at that record. You never need another record to decide
it. That is what makes the feature *local*, and locality is the hinge of
everything below.

```turbulance
item chebi = {
    name: "chebi",
    capabilities: ["descendants_of", "formula", "charge", "inchikey"]
}

item brenda = {
    name: "brenda",
    capabilities: ["ec_number", "organism", "k_cat", "substrate"]
}

item rhea = {
    name: "rhea",
    capabilities: ["ec_number", "reaction", "participants", "direction"]
}

print(chebi.name, len(chebi.capabilities), "features")
print(brenda.name, len(brenda.capabilities), "features")
print(rhea.name, len(rhea.capabilities), "features")
```

```output
chebi 4 features
brenda 4 features
rhea 4 features

chebi = {name: chebi, capabilities: [descendants_of, formula, charge, inchikey]}
brenda = {name: brenda, capabilities: [ec_number, organism, k_cat, substrate]}
rhea = {name: rhea, capabilities: [ec_number, reaction, participants, direction]}
```

Three sources, three declarations. Nothing has been fetched. No record has been
read. These are statements *about* the sources, made by whoever wrote them down.

## Containment is the whole check

A step against a source declares what it requires. The step is **well-capability**
when its requirements are contained in the source's declared capabilities.

That is the entire test. One membership check per required feature.

```turbulance
funxn has(xs, target):
    item found = false
    for each x in xs:
        given x == target:
            found = true
    return found

funxn missing(required, available):
    item gap = []
    for each r in required:
        given not has(available, r):
            gap = gap + [r]
    return gap

funxn contained(required, available):
    return len(missing(required, available)) == 0

item request_a = ["ec_number", "k_cat"]
item request_b = ["ec_number", "k_cat", "ph_optimum"]

print("A against brenda:", contained(request_a, brenda.capabilities))
print("B against brenda:", contained(request_b, brenda.capabilities))
print("B is missing:", missing(request_b, brenda.capabilities))
```

```output
A against brenda: true
B against brenda: false
B is missing: [ph_optimum]

has = closure(has)
missing = closure(missing)
contained = closure(contained)
request_a = [ec_number, k_cat]
request_b = [ec_number, k_cat, ph_optimum]
```

Two things about that result are worth saying out loud.

**First: it is decidable, and cheap.** The cost is one membership test per
required feature -- linear in the size of the feature alphabet, and independent
of how much data the source holds. A source with four records and a source with
four billion records take the same time to fail this check.

**Second, and this is the one people miss: no request was issued.** Request B
was refused *before the network*. Not refused after a timeout, not refused after
returning nothing -- refused without asking. The diagnosis is `ph_optimum`, a
named feature, not an error string.

That is what a schema buys you. It is a real thing to buy.

## The verdict is not a boolean

Here is where the host query language departs from the script everybody writes.

In an ordinary script, a step succeeds or it fails, and failure is one bit wide.
A step that returned nothing because the source was down, because the query was
malformed, because the budget ran out, and because the answer genuinely is
"there are none" all look identical from the outside.

The host assigns one of **six verdicts**, by rules applied in a fixed order:

```turbulance
funxn verdict(inputs_ok, is_contained, budget_ok, completed, nonempty):
    given not inputs_ok:
        return "starved"
    given not is_contained:
        return "unsupported"
    given not budget_ok:
        return "refused"
    given not completed:
        return "exhausted"
    given not nonempty:
        return "empty"
    return "answer"

print("R1", verdict(false, false, false, false, false))
print("R2", verdict(true,  false, false, false, false))
print("R3", verdict(true,  true,  false, false, false))
print("R4", verdict(true,  true,  true,  false, false))
print("R5", verdict(true,  true,  true,  true,  false))
print("R6", verdict(true,  true,  true,  true,  true))
```

```output
R1 starved
R2 unsupported
R3 refused
R4 exhausted
R5 empty
R6 answer

verdict = closure(verdict)
```

Read the encoding, not just the output. Turbulance has no `elif`, and two
`given` branches in sequence do not exclude one another -- tutorial 6 relied on
exactly that when it let four branches all fire into one motion.

Here the early `return` is doing the work. Each rule is reached **only when
every earlier cause has been excluded**, which is not a convenience: the paper
calls the order *normative*, and it is what makes a verdict attributive. A step
labelled `empty` is a step that was well-capability, was affordable, completed
in time, and genuinely found nothing. You can act on that. You cannot act on
`false`.

Note which rule is which. **R2 is the capability check** -- the schema layer,
deciding from declarations alone. **R1 is not**: a step is starved when its
*input* fell short, which is a fact about what flowed through the plan, and
no schema predicts it. Hold on to that; tutorial 9 is about R1.

One caution about R2, paid off in tutorial 11: `unsupported` as written here
is doing the work of two verdicts. It fires both when a source lacks a feature
another source has, and when the feature is absent from the federation's
vocabulary entirely -- and those license completely different next actions.
Take the six as provisional until then.

## One payload rule

```turbulance
funxn step_result(v, rows):
    given v == "answer":
        return {verdict: v, payload: rows, carries: true}
    return {verdict: v, payload: [], carries: false}

item ok = step_result("answer", ["EC 1.1.1.1", "EC 1.1.1.2"])
item no = step_result("unsupported", ["EC 1.1.1.1"])

print(ok.verdict, "carries", ok.carries, ok.payload)
print(no.verdict, "carries", no.carries, no.payload)
```

```output
answer carries true [EC 1.1.1.1, EC 1.1.1.2]
unsupported carries false []

step_result = closure(step_result)
ok = {verdict: answer, payload: [EC 1.1.1.1, EC 1.1.1.2], carries: true}
no = {verdict: unsupported, payload: [], carries: false}
```

Only `answer` carries a result. A verdict that shipped a partial payload
alongside a failure would put back exactly the ambiguity the six verdicts exist
to remove -- you would be back to inspecting the rows to guess what happened.

## Honesty is assumed, never verified

One limitation, stated here rather than buried, because it is inherited
unchanged and it is not fixable at this layer.

`brenda.capabilities` is a **declaration**. Nothing in the containment check
verifies it. A source that declares `k_cat` and has no such field passes every
check this tutorial performs, and fails later, somewhere else, for reasons that
will look like a different problem.

```turbulance
item honest = {name: "src-a", capabilities: ["ec_number", "k_cat"]}
item liar   = {name: "src-b", capabilities: ["ec_number", "k_cat"]}

print("indistinguishable at the interface:",
      contained(["k_cat"], honest.capabilities) == contained(["k_cat"], liar.capabilities))
```

```output
indistinguishable at the interface: true

honest = {name: src-a, capabilities: [ec_number, k_cat]}
liar = {name: src-b, capabilities: [ec_number, k_cat]}
```

Over-declaration is **indistinguishable from correct declaration at the
interface**. This is true of LinkML too, and of JSON Schema, SHACL and OWL: a
schema is a promise about records, and the promise is checkable against records
you have, not against the source that will send you the next one.

So the containment check is a check on *declarations*, and it is honest about
being one.

## Where this stops

Everything above is decided from `Cap(Src)` and the request. That is a real and
useful layer, and for a large class of questions it is the whole job: a dataset
catalogue listing, a metadata harvest, a FAIR-compliance check. For those,
adopting LinkML unmodified is the right engineering choice and extending it
would be cost for no gain.

The next tutorial is about a question this layer cannot reach -- not because the
schema is too small, but because of what kind of property the question is
asking about.

Next: [09-what-the-schema-cannot-say.ndo](09-what-the-schema-cannot-say.ndo).
